+# Specialty-Major View by Inverting the Veterinarian Association
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The specialty directory publishes every specialty with the veterinarians holding it. A specialty no veterinarian holds still appears.
+
+The persisted association runs one way. `Vet` maps a many-to-many to `Specialty`; `Specialty` holds no reference back. Nothing in the model answers "who holds this specialty", and no repository reads specialties at all.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Bidirectional mapping** — add an inverse many-to-many collection to `Specialty`, mapped by the owning side on `Vet`.
+2. **Per-specialty query** — read the specialties, then query the veterinarians of each one in turn.
+3. **Inversion in a read model** — read both sides once, and pair them in an immutable type outside persistence.
+
+## Decision
+
+We choose option 3. A `SpecialtyRepository` reads every specialty, `VetRepository` reads every veterinarian with its specialties already fetched, and an immutable `SpecialtyDirectory` read model pairs them.
+
+`SpecialtyDirectory` also fixes the presentation order in Java, by specialty name and then by veterinarian last and first name. Ordering therefore does not depend on database collation, which diverges across the three supported vendors.
+
+The two sides are paired by persisted identity, not by reference and not by name. `VetRepository.findAll` is cached and the specialty read is not, so one row reaches the pairing as two distinct instances. Neither `Vet` nor `Specialty` declares value equality, so reference pairing would silently find no holders. The `specialties.name` column carries an index but no uniqueness constraint, so name pairing would merge two same-named specialties.
+
+`SpecialtyDirectory` declares no `equals` or `hashCode`. Its contents are persisted entities equal by identity, so value equality would promise more than those contents can deliver.
+
+Option 1 is rejected because `Vet` fetches its specialties eagerly and open-session-in-view is disabled. An inverse collection would have to be eager too, making every specialty read pull the veterinarian graph back. It also mutates a persisted type for a read-only page.
+
+Option 2 is rejected because it issues one query per specialty and bypasses the cached whole-collection read on `VetRepository`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The persisted model is unchanged, and the read shape lives in one immutable type that is unit-testable without Spring or a database.
+
+The pairing runs in memory over the whole specialty and veterinarian sets. Both are small lookup-scale collections here. A clinic with a large roster would need a different read path.
+
+The specialty read is not cached. `CacheConfiguration` declares only the veterinarian cache, and a `@Cacheable` name it does not declare fails at runtime. Adding a second cache would also add a second uninvalidated cache, which is already an open question for the veterinarian one.
+
+Order is asserted in Java rather than delegated to the store, so it diverges from `PetTypeRepository`, which orders in the query.
+
+`SpecialtyDirectory` diverges from the *Value object* row in [`architecture-principles.md`](../architecture-principles.md#pattern-catalog), which requires equality by value. The divergence is recorded here rather than left for a reviewer to find. It is bounded to this type: a read model whose contents are persisted entities cannot deliver value equality, and no other type gains an exemption.
+
+Pairing by identity binds the directory to persisted rows. An unsaved `Specialty` has a null id and would pair with every other unsaved one, so the read model is valid only over stored state.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-SPC-001
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md#contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the contracts realizing this decision
+- [2026-07-31-relational-persistence-with-spring-data-jpa.md](2026-07-31-relational-persistence-with-spring-data-jpa.md) — mapping on the domain types
+- [2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md](2026-07-31-feature-package-organization.md) — feature packages without a service layer
